course: dictionary
lesson: 3826
title: Formal and informal words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Formally means in a polite, serious and correct way, the way people speak or write for important occasions. Think of a letter to a head teacher compared with a text to a friend. Informal is the relaxed way we talk at home.
explain: Formally can also mean officially, done in the right way by the right people. «In 2008 the Prime Minister formally apologised.» The apology was made officially, seriously and properly, not just said in passing.
explain: Formally can describe clothes too: «dressed formally» means in smart clothes for a special event. To work out the meaning, look at the place, the people and the kind of words used. Ask yourself: is this serious and polite, or relaxed and casual?
fact: Formally means in a polite, serious, correct way.
fact: Formally can mean officially, as in «formally announced».
fact: Formally can mean in proper dress for a special event, as in «dressed formally».
fact: The opposite of formally is informally, which means casually or in a relaxed way.
fact: Formal writing, such as letters and reports, is written formally. Check a word by asking if it would fit a letter to a head teacher.
exercise: «In 2008 the Prime Minister formally apologised.» What does formally mean here? | *Officially and seriously | Very quickly | Casually, as a joke | Quietly, in a whisper
exercise: Which word means the opposite of formally? | Politely | Seriously | Officially | *Informally
exercise: Which sentence is written formally? | *«Dear Mrs Patel, thank you very much for the lovely cake.» | «cake = good, ta» | «lol the cake was yum» | «Hey, thanks lots for the cake!»
test: Which would be «dressed formally» for a wedding? | Old jeans with a hole in the knee | *A smart suit and polished shoes | Pyjamas and slippers | Muddy football kit
test: The new library was ___ opened by the mayor in a speech to the town. Which word fits best? | casually | curtly | *formally | barely
exam: Which sentence is the most formal? | The window got broken | The window got broke | *The window was broken | The window bust
